<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<addon id="service.rpi-gpio-spdif"
       name="S/PDIF output on GPIO21 (ALSA plugin)"
       version="@VERSION@"
       provider-name="ehb">
  <requires>
    <import addon="xbmc.python" version="3.0.1"/>
  </requires>
  <extension point="xbmc.service" library="default.py"/>
  <extension point="xbmc.addon.metadata">
    <summary lang="en_GB">Optical/coaxial S/PDIF out on a Raspberry Pi GPIO without a kernel driver, with AC3/DTS passthrough</summary>
    <description lang="en_GB">An ALSA plugin encodes S/PDIF (biphase-mark) in software and plays it through the stock I2S driver of the kernel, so the signal appears on GPIO21 (pin 40). The add-on brings a small device tree overlay that claims only GPIO21 (Pi 0-5; the Pi 5 is untested); where it does not apply, it falls back to the stock overlay hifiberry-dac. Wire a TOSLINK transmitter module (or a coax driver) to GPIO21, 5V and GND. Kodi gets the audio device "hdmi_bmc" (shown as "S/PDIF on GPIO21") and offers AC3/DTS passthrough on it.[CR][CR]Depends on the architecture (aarch64, LibreELEC on a 64-bit Raspberry Pi), not on the kernel version. Do not run it together with the kernel driver add-on driver.audio.rpi-gpio-spdif or with raspdif.</description>
    <platform>linux</platform>
    <license>GPL-2.0-only</license>
    <source>https://github.com/ehb/rpi-gpio-spdif</source>
  </extension>
</addon>
